{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794153506,"nonce":"Rzmq4OUMkLxWnEwuCJk/iv3Mdi0=","message":"2026-11-08, sustained load on the iPhone 12 Pro Max, after the core shader specialisation.\n\nA 68 s capture of continuous scrolling (buffered logging, so it does not disturb frame timing): 0 to 30 s steady 60.0 fps with the surface wait at 0 and CPU render time 1 ms a frame; from 30 s the surface wait rises to about 10 ms and fps decays to 44 to 56 while CPU time stays flat. The GPU slows as the SoC heats, so the frame has too little headroom. Scrolling captures taken after many back-to-back test launches showed 26 fps for the same code, which was also this. A Metal trace of the hot phone puts the GPU frame at about 20 ms where it fits in 16.7 cool.\n\nNext biggest cost, from the earlier flat-fill experiments: the shadow pipeline (about 10 to 12 ms of the original 34 on the cool phone). The shadow shader needs the same feature switches as the core one, or shadows need to come off the hot path. mobile/example already drops the card shadow; it now drops the field and filled-button shadows too (flat controls, as on iOS and Material), which removes the shadow draws from its screens. Retained scroll layer (unchanged content shaded once, scrolled as a texture) remains the larger fix.\n\nTest protocol for anyone profiling on a phone: let it cool between runs, and judge by a steady-state window, not a single launch.","files":null}]}